  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few strong to severe thunderstorms are\npossible from central Virginia to southern Maryland this \nafternoon.\n\nAs of 10 AM, radar imagery shows showers and thunderstorms \npassing just to the south of the area across southeastern \nVirginia. Clouds and a few showers on the northwestern fringe of\nthat activity have made it into St. Mary's County. Further \nnorth, skies remain mostly sunny across the majority of the \nforecast area. \n\nA prefrontal trough will switch winds to westerly through much\nof the day before northerly winds bring the true airmass change\ntonight. The cold front likely overtakes the trough at some\npoint but will temporarily stall this afternoon, likely\nextending across central Virginia to southern Maryland. Dew\npoints will remain in the 70s in this area, supporting some\ninstability to go along with the wind shear provided by the\nupper trough. Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms\nare forecast along and southeast of the front, although there \nremains some uncertainty in coverage and intensity. Given the \nshear in place, there is some potential for severe \nthunderstorms, with damaging winds being the primary threat. \nThunderstorms may also be repetitive along the slow moving \nfront, but the expected fast individual storm motions will \nlikely preclude any flood threat.\n\nThe front will drop south of the area this evening, but an area\nof low pressure may ride along it. This could result in\nadditional showers spreading across central Virginia and\nsouthern Maryland through the night.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, highs today will be in the 70s to\nlower 80s west of I-81 and mid to upper 80s to the east.\nLows tonight will drop into the 50s and 60s, and some of the\nsheltered high elevation valleys could reach the 40s.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected Thursday through Saturday.\n\nA sprawling area of high pressure will build to our north for \nThursday and Friday, before shifting off the New England \nCoastline on Saturday. Northerly flow ahead of the approaching \nhigh will advect a much cooler and drier airmass into the region\nfor Thursday. Well below normal temperatures and dewpoints are \nforecast for late July, with high temperatures only in the upper\n70s/low 80s. Dewpoints in the 50s will also make it feel much \nless humid than is typical for July. Mostly sunny skies will \naccompany the well below normal temperatures/dewpoints. \nTemperatures will stay well below normal for Friday and Saturday\nwithin easterly flow. Some moisture could be drawn northward\nfrom the stalled frontal zone across the southeastern states\nduring this time. The current forecast has some low rain chances\nboth days, mainly in southern portions of the CWA and along the\nAllegheny Mountains.\n\nBoth temperatures and dewpoints begin to rise again on Sunday \nas flow turns southerly, with highs in the mid to upper 80s and \ndewpoints in the 60s. The return of higher dewpoint air should \nlead to slightly greater chances for afternoon/evening showers \nand thunderstorms, but most locations will likely still stay \ndry.\n\nForecast confidence decreases substantially by Monday and Tuesday, \nbut chances for showers and thunderstorms may further increase as \nmultiple shortwave disturbances move through in northwesterly flow \naloft. Depending on how the forecast details evolve, there could be \na conditional threat for severe thunderstorms on either Monday or \nTuesday.",
